By the Extreme Value Theorem, $f$ attains its maximum and its minimum on $[a, b]$: there are $x_(m), x_(M) in [a, b]$ with
$
  sup_([a, b]) f = f(x_M), quad inf_([a, b]) f = f(x_m).
$
If an extremum lies in the open interval, say $x_m in (a, b)$, then $(f(x_m + h) - f(x_m))/(h)$ has the same sign as $h$; taking the limits along $h -> 0^+$ and $h -> 0^-$ forces $f'(x_m) = 0$. Similarly for a maximum $x_M in (a, b)$, where the difference quotient has sign opposite to $h$.

If $f$ is not constant, then either $f(a) < f(b)$ or $f(a) > f(b)$, so at least one of $x_m$, $x_M$ cannot sit at the endpoints and must lie in $(a, b)$. If $f$ is constant the result is trivial.
